issues.body:
## Three independent bugs in core tools

Found while reviewing `packages/coding-agent/src/core/tools/`. Fix branch: https://github.com/IKEASven69/pi/tree/fix/tool-bytes-limit-truncation

### Bug 1: write.ts reports wrong byte count

`write.ts:222` uses `content.length` (UTF-16 code units) instead of actual UTF-8 bytes:

```typescript
// Before
text: `Successfully wrote ${content.length} bytes to ${path}`
// After
text: `Successfully wrote ${Buffer.byteLength(content, "utf-8")} bytes to ${path}`
```

Writing 1000 Chinese characters reports "1000 bytes" when the actual UTF-8 size is 3000 bytes. This misleads the model about file size, affecting subsequent offset/limit decisions in read calls.

### Bug 2: find.ts false "results limit reached" warning

`find.ts:184` uses `>=` to detect limit overflow:

```typescript
const resultLimitReached = relativized.length >= effectiveLimit;
```

Since fd is called with `--max-results N`, any search returning exactly N results triggers a false truncation warning. The model then wastefully re-runs the same search with `limit=2N`.

Fix: request `N+1` results from fd, then check `> effectiveLimit`. If we got more than N, trim back to N and report truncation; if exactly N, no warning.

### Bug 3: truncateLine splits surrogate pairs (emoji)

`truncate.ts:275` uses `line.slice(0, maxChars)` which cuts by UTF-16 code unit. If the cut point falls inside a surrogate pair (emoji, rare CJK), it orphans the high surrogate, producing U+FFFD in grep output:

```typescript
// Before
return { text: `${line.slice(0, maxChars)}... [truncated]`, wasTruncated: true };
// After: check if cut point is a low surrogate, back up by one
if (cut > 0) {
    const code = line.charCodeAt(cut);
    if (code >= 0xDC00 && code <= 0xDFFF) cut -= 1;
}
```

## Verification

All three are minimal, localized changes with no behavioral side effects beyond the fix itself. Asking for maintainer approval to open a PR per the contribution guidelines.

### What happened?

The `find` tool silently returns no results when the glob pattern uses Windows path separators, e.g. `src\**\*.ts`. An agent (or user) copying a native Windows path pattern gets nothing back — no error — and can wrongly conclude the files don't exist.

Follow-up to #6817: the core bug there (forward-slash patterns like `src/**/*.ts` not matching on Windows) is fixed on `main`, but backslash patterns were never supported and remain broken.

### Reproduction (verified through pi's real code path, Windows 11 + fd 10.5.0)

A temporary test using the harness from `test/suite/regressions/3302-find-path-glob.test.ts` (`createFindToolDefinition` + `execute({ pattern })`) against a temp tree containing `src/foo/bar/example.spec.ts`:

- `runFind("src/**/*.spec.ts")` → `["src/foo/bar/example.spec.ts"]`
- `runFind(String.raw`src\**\*.spec.ts`)` → `[]`

fd-level check of the args pi currently builds: `fd --glob 'src\**\*.ts' <root>` exits 0 with no output; `fd --glob --full-path '**\src\**\*.ts'` also matches nothing, while the forward-slash equivalents match — fd needs the pattern normalized to `/` before it can match.

### Root cause

`packages/coding-agent/src/core/tools/find.ts` (fd-backed branch of `execute`): full-path mode is gated on `pattern.includes("/")` only:

```ts
if (pattern.includes("/")) { ... args.push("--full-path") ... }
```

A backslash pattern never enters that branch, so it is passed to `fd --glob` in basename mode. fd globs use `/` as the path separator (`\` is not one in glob patterns on Windows), so a pattern containing `\` can never match any basename → silent empty result.

### Impact

On Windows, path patterns authored with native separators (a model mirroring a path from shell/explorer output, or a user typing `src\**\*.ts`) silently return nothing. `find` reports no matches, the model can conclude code does not exist, and the agent wastes turns — the same class of silent failure #6817 had before its fix.

### Fix (implemented and verified locally)

In the fd branch, treat `\` as a separator on win32, normalize it to `/`, then reuse the existing full-path handling unchanged:

```ts
let effectivePattern = pattern;
const hasPathSeparator =
    pattern.includes("/") || (process.platform === "win32" && pattern.includes("\\"));
if (hasPathSeparator) {
    args.push("--full-path");
    // Windows path patterns may use native backslashes; unify them to '/'
    // so the prefix and separator-class rewrite below handle both forms.
    if (process.platform === "win32") effectivePattern = effectivePattern.replaceAll("\\", "/");
    if (!effectivePattern.startsWith("/") && !effectivePattern.startsWith("**/") && effectivePattern !== "**") {
        effectivePattern = `**/${effectivePattern}`;
    }
    // fd matches full paths using native separators on Windows.
    if (process.platform === "win32")
        effectivePattern = effectivePattern.replaceAll("/", String.raw`[/\\]`);
}
```

Guarded to win32 only: on POSIX `\` is a legitimate glob escape character and file names may contain it, so behavior there is untouched. Pure-basename patterns (`*.ts`) and forward-slash patterns keep current behavior.

Local verification after the fix (isolated HOME, real fd 10.5.0):
- the failing backslash test passes (backslash ≡ forward-slash result set)
- `**\parent\child\*` ≡ `**/parent/child/*`
- mixed separators `src\**/*.spec.ts` ≡ `src/**/*.spec.ts`
- all 4 existing tests in `3302-find-path-glob.test.ts` still pass

### Test plan for the PR

Add win32-gated cases (`it.runIf(process.platform === "win32")` — on Linux a backslash pattern legitimately means something else, so the cases must not run there) to the existing `3302-find-path-glob.test.ts` harness, asserting backslash patterns return the same set as their forward-slash equivalents.

Happy to open the PR once approved.

### What happened?

When a tool's parameter schema uses a relative `$id` and a `$ref` to one of its own definitions, Pi resolves the `$id` twice. It cannot find the definition, so it rejects valid arguments and the tool never runs.

For example, this schema defines an integer parameter named `count`:

```json
{
  "$id": "schemas/tool.json",
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "count": { "$ref": "#/$defs/count" }
  },
  "required": ["count"],
  "$defs": {
    "count": { "type": "integer" }
  }
}
```

`$ref: "#/$defs/count"` refers to the integer definition inside the current schema. Therefore, `{"count": 2}` should pass validation.

But Pi resolves the relative `$id` twice:

```text
Correct: schemas/tool.json
Wrong:   schemas/schemas/tool.json
```

Pi then looks for the definition at the wrong address, fails to resolve `$ref`, and rejects otherwise valid arguments.

In a controlled CLI test:

- Both `{"count": 2}` and `{"count": 3}` fail with `root: invalid`.
- The tool's `execute()` is never called.
- Other tools that do not use this schema pattern still work.
- Despite the failed tool calls, the CLI exits with code `0`.

Removing only this `$id`, or replacing it with an absolute URL, allows the same arguments to pass validation. However, removing `$id` is not a general solution, since other references may rely on it to resolve their addresses.

### Steps to reproduce

In a checkout of the commit below, create `repro-relative-id.mjs` in the repository root and run `node repro-relative-id.mjs`:

```js
import { compile } from "./packages/schema/src/index.ts";

const schema = {
  $id: "schemas/tool.json",
  type: "object",
  properties: { count: { $ref: "#/$defs/count" } },
  required: ["count"],
  $defs: { count: { type: "integer" } },
};

console.log(
  compile(schema, { unresolvedRefs: "reject" }).check({ count: 2 })
);
// Expected: true
// Actual: false

compile(schema);
// Throws: Unresolved $ref #/$defs/count
// The incorrect lookup address contains: schemas/schemas/tool.json
```

This was found through controlled testing, not a production incident. A separate CLI test confirmed the effect on tool calls: extension discovery was disabled with `-ne`, and only the test tool and local faux provider were explicitly loaded. No real model API was called, and remote MCP integration was not tested.

### Expected behavior

The relative root `$id` should be resolved only once, and `$ref` should find the definition inside the current schema. Valid arguments such as `{"count": 2}` should pass validation and the tool should execute normally.

I'd be happy to submit a minimal fix and add a regression test, pending maintainer approval.

### Version

Source commit `c9f4c7b9fcd0f3e6b5bbc35c7c9b6cda35580eb8` on `main`; Node.js 24.20.0; macOS.

### What happened?

With stdin kept open, an idle `--mode rpc` process stays alive after an extension calls `ctx.shutdown()` asynchronously, for example from a timer callback. The shutdown request is not processed until another RPC command arrives.

The [RPC shutdown handler](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/f09d01b85e0384647fcab6779d35ab9dabe04be3/packages/coding-agent/src/modes/rpc/rpc-mode.ts#L345-L347) only sets `shutdownRequested`; it checks the flag after stdin commands or `agent_settled`. An idle request has neither trigger.

### Steps to reproduce

Save `shutdown-probe.mjs` in an empty directory:

```js
export default function (pi) {
  pi.on("session_start", (_, ctx) => {
    setTimeout(() => {
      process.stderr.write("shutdown requested\n");
      ctx.shutdown();
    }, 200);
  });
  pi.on("session_shutdown", () => process.stderr.write("session_shutdown\n"));
}
```

Run with stdin left open:

```sh
PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR="$(mktemp -d)" pi --mode rpc --no-session -ne -e ./shutdown-probe.mjs
```

After `shutdown requested`, the process remains alive and `session_shutdown` is not emitted. Enter `{"type":"get_state"}` followed by Enter: the query succeeds, `session_shutdown` is emitted, and Pi exits with code 0. Reproduced with isolated configuration and only the probe extension enabled.

### Expected behavior

An idle `ctx.shutdown()` request should perform orderly shutdown without requiring another stdin command.

