### Summary

On Windows 11 with OpenClaw 2026.9.7 (npm latest, published 2026-09-30), enabling ANY non-bundled plugin loaded via `plugins.load.paths` triggers a source-capture staging build under `<state-dir>/tmp/plugin-captures/<uuid>/captures/openclaw-plugin-build-*/`. For plugins whose install layout contains `node_modules` (the normal `npm install --prefix` shape), the staged build re-materializes the plugin's own package as a real directory `package-0/node_modules/<plugin-self>` plus peer junctions, and the copy does not converge — the gateway never reaches healthy and the plugin instance ends in forced retirement. Native binaries are not the trigger: a zero-native plugin is captured identically. After manual surgery (remove the self entry and all junctions; flatten deps inside the plugin root), the SAME plugin's capture converges at 14.6 MB and the gateway boots healthy. A third case with a large dependency tree (openclaw-mem0, 290 MB / ~325 packages) shows a second failure face even without any self-loop: the capture grew to 612 MB after 24 minutes, monotonic, self-repeat counter = 0 (combinatorial flattening blow-up, not recursion) — no startup budget can cover it, and the health monitor escalates into a restart loop (same collision family as #162211). Stock/bundled plugins and `__openclaw-generation__` artifacts are never captured and never affected.

### Steps to reproduce

1. Windows 11 (10.0.26200) x64, Node v24.16.0. `OPENCLAW_CONFIG_PATH` and `OPENCLAW_STATE_DIR` redirected to `D:\ZJClaw\state`. Start the gateway headless exactly as our launcher does: `node openclaw.mjs --port 24029`.
2. Prepare an external plugin at `D:\ZJClaw\extensions\<plugin>` installed via `npm install --prefix D:\ZJClaw\extensions\<plugin>`, so the directory contains `node_modules/<plugin-self>` and a peer `node_modules/openclaw` junction.
3. In openclaw.json: add the directory to `plugins.load.paths`, set `plugins.entries.<id>.enabled=true` (and `plugins.slots.memory=<id>` for memory plugins), then start the gateway.
4. Watch the capture tree with a depth-converged size sampler (script below): `node capture-watch.cjs D:\ZJClaw\state\tmp\plugin-captures <plugin-name-fragment>`.

Reproduced three times with three different plugins:

- `@memtensor/memos-local-openclaw-plugin` 1.0.10 (better-sqlite3 + prebuilds): capture recurses (self-copy + junction re-expansion); gateway never healthy. Three install channels tried (npm tarball, source link, dep-slimmed path) — nesting recurs in all.
- `openbmb-clawxmemory` 0.1.5 (ZERO native, only @sinclair/typebox in node_modules, 13 MB): captured identically → proves the trigger is "non-bundled", not native deps.
- `openclaw-mem0` (mem0ai SDK, 290 MB / ~325 packages): manual gateway bypassing our watchdog; capture reached 612 MB at 24 min and was still climbing (maxSelfRepeatsInOnePath = 0 → pure flattening explosion).

```js
// capture-watch.cjs — depth-converged size sampler, counts self-name repeats within one path
const fs = require('fs'), path = require('path');
const [root, frag] = process.argv.slice(2);
let prev = 0;
setInterval(() => {
  let size = 0, worstSelf = 0;
  const stack = [[root, 0]];
  while (stack.length) {
    const [dir, selfCount] = stack.pop();
    let ents; try { ents = fs.readdirSync(dir, { withFileTypes: true }); } catch { continue; }
    for (const e of ents) {
      const p = path.join(dir, e.name);
      if (e.isSymbolicLink()) continue;               // real dirs only
      if (e.isDirectory()) {
        const sc = selfCount + (frag && e.name.includes(frag) ? 1 : 0);
        if (sc > worstSelf) worstSelf = sc;
        stack.push([p, sc]);
      } else try { size += fs.statSync(p).size; } catch {}
    }
  }
  const mb = size / 1048576;
  console.log(new Date().toISOString(), `sizeMB=${mb.toFixed(1)} maxSelfRepeatsInOnePath=${worstSelf} jumpMB=${(mb-prev).toFixed(1)}`);
  prev = mb;
}, 30000);
```

### Expected behavior

The capture/staging build converges to a stable size bounded by the plugin's source tree (same as after our surgery: 14.6 MB, gateway healthy), and enabling an external memory plugin (`slots.memory` + `entries.enabled=true`) makes the gateway reach `ready` within its health budget. Reference points: (a) bundled/stock plugins (deepseek, qwen) are never captured and enable instantly; (b) our surgically flattened variant of the very same plugin (self entry removed, junctions removed) converges and boots healthy on the same 2026.9.7 build — so the pipeline CAN converge; the gap is in handling `node_modules`-bearing layouts.

### Actual behavior

- Staged build materializes the plugin's own package inside its own staging: gateway log shows real paths like `<state>\tmp\plugin-captures\<uuid>\captures\openclaw-plugin-build-fXMI3l\package-0\node_modules\memos-local-openclaw-plugin`, with peer deps flattened into sibling `package-2\node_modules\...`; dereferencing re-expands the tree and never converges.
- `openclaw-plugin-build-*` dirs appear on EVERY registry inspection/reload (per UUID, multiple builds), growing unbounded on disk while the gateway never reaches healthy; the instance ends in `[plugins/cleanup] ... forced retirement after 5000ms`.
- Large npm trees: monotonic growth past 612 MB in 24 min with self-repeat = 0; under the standard health monitor this turns into kill/respawn loops (see #162211).

### OpenClaw version

2026.9.7 (npm latest, published 2026-09-30)

### Operating system

Windows 11 (10.0.26200) x64 — Node v24.16.0 (bundled runtime)

### Install method

npm (`openclaw` package under a runtime node_modules); gateway spawned headless via `node openclaw.mjs --port <n>` by an Electron launcher with its own watchdog (health probe 4 s × 8 → SIGTERM → respawn, first-attempt budget 240 s). OPENCLAW_STATE_DIR / OPENCLAW_CONFIG_PATH redirected off C:.

### Model

deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash (not implicated — the deadlock happens at plugin admission, before any model call)

### Provider / routing chain

openclaw -> https://api.deepseek.com (direct; unrelated to this bug)

### Additional provider/model setup details

5 agents configured; external plugins registered via plugins.load.paths pointing at D:\ZJClaw\extensions\*; memory slot plugins tested: @memtensor/memos-local-openclaw-plugin 1.0.10, openbmb-clawxmemory 0.1.5, openclaw-mem0. All currently disabled as workaround; file-layer memory via bundled core still works. No API keys included here.

### Logs

```
2026-09-30T22:31:59.966+08:00 [plugins] memos-local: better-sqlite3 resolved outside plugin dir: D:\ZJClaw\state\tmp\plugin-captures\45d69efc-...\captures\openclaw-plugin-build-fXMI3l\package-2\node_modules\better-sqlite3\lib\index.js
2026-09-30T22:31:59.969+08:00 [plugins] memos-local: better-sqlite3 not found in D:\ZJClaw\state\tmp\plugin-captures\45d69efc-...\captures\openclaw-plugin-build-fXMI3l\package-0\node_modules\memos-local-openclaw-plugin, attempting auto-rebuild ...
2026-09-30T22:32:01.471+08:00 [gateway] [plugins] plugin failed during register: Error: better-sqlite3 native module not found ... (plugin=memos-local-openclaw-plugin, source=D:\ZJClaw\extensions\memos-local-openclaw-plugin\index.ts)
2026-10-01T11:40:00.503+08:00 [plugins] [clawxmemory] skills loaded from D:\ZJClaw\state\tmp\plugin-captures\5291377a-...\captures\openclaw-plugin-build-NinHuP\package-0\node_modules\openbmb-clawxmemory\skills\
2026-10-01T11:40:42.988+08:00 [plugins/cleanup] Plugin openbmb-clawxmemory forced retirement after 5000ms: 1 still-running call(s), 0 retained consumer(s); resource cleanup remains pending.
capture-watch series (openclaw-mem0, manual gateway, no watchdog): 2026-10-01T03:15 sizeMB=291.8 -> 03:27 sizeMB=455.3 -> 03:39 sizeMB=612.4, maxSelfRepeatsInOnePath=0, jumpMB~+20/30s sustained, still climbing when terminated at 24 min.
```

### Screenshots, recordings, and evidence

Evidence = the two excerpted log families above (timestamps 2026-09-30 / 10-01, gateway-console.log, unredacted paths preserved) + the capture-watch console series. Happy to attach the full sampler output and our staged-layout diff (before/after surgery: junctions+self entry vs flattened root) on request.

### Impact and severity

High for plugin-based memory: on 2026.9.7 Windows, NO third-party memory plugin can be enabled at all (memos: non-converging recursion; mem0: explosion beyond any boot budget; clawx-family: only survivable via manual surgery that ordinary users cannot perform). Gateway never reaches healthy while a captured plugin is enabled → launcher/health-monitor restart storms (collision family of #162211) and unbounded disk growth under `<state-dir>/tmp/plugin-captures`. Bundled plugins unaffected, so scope = exactly "external plugins with node_modules-bearing layouts".

### Additional information

Root-cause hypothesis: the load.paths/extensions staging branch walks `node_modules` INCLUDING the plugin's own package directory and dereferences junctions, whereas the npm-project branch described in closed #155803 (superseded by open #152872) explicitly skips `openclaw`/`@openclaw/plugin-sdk` and `copy()` skips `node_modules`. The asymmetry between these two staging branches is where the fix likely lives.

Suggested fixes:
1) Self-exclusion: skip the staged entry whose manifest name equals the plugin's own package name (or link instead of copy).
2) Cycle guard: traverse by real path (volumeSerial+fileId on Windows); never descend an already-visited real directory.
3) Junction policy: preserve reparse points as reparse points inside captures; do not expand them.
4) Budget + graceful degradation: per-capture byte/time ceilings; on breach fail ONLY that plugin with an actionable error while the gateway still reaches healthy (decouple from #162211 restart-loop family).
5) Content-hash capture store so the same dependency subtree is materialized once per generation (also shrinks #161147).

Related: closed leak family #152689 #154571 #154973 #156191 (many-dir accumulation, not single-build non-convergence); open same-collision #162211 #152981 #159499 #152872 #161147.

Workaround we validated on 2026.9.7: flatten the plugin root (body at root, no self entry, no junctions, deps inside own node_modules) → capture converges (14.6 MB) and gateway boots healthy.

prs.body:
Closes #169357

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes: history replay re-encodes every stored photo or screenshot wider than 2048 px on every model request of the next 3 to 10 turns. A 12 MP phone photo costs about 0.9 to 1.2 s per request, so a follow-up turn with several tool rounds waits seconds longer for each retained image.

## User Impact

After someone sends a phone photo or a large screenshot to a vision model, later turns no longer pay for re-encoding it. In a running Gateway, a follow-up turn with 6 tool rounds after one 12 MP photo went from 7.1 s to 0.94 s. In the replay benchmark, 8 tool rounds after one photo went from 8.0 s to 0.17 s, and after three photos from 24.1 s to 0.45 s. The image bytes sent to the model are byte-identical to `main`. There is no configuration, transcript, or public API change.

## Why This Change Was Made

Every model request runs the history transform that `attempt-setup.ts` installs: `installHistoryImagePruneContextTransform` → `hydratePromptMediaMessages` → `loadMediaFromRef` → `loadWebMedia` → `optimizeImageBufferForWebMedia`. Persisted user turns keep media facts, not bytes. Originals over the preferred 2048 px side are not preserved, so `processor.encode` ran the full side/quality search again each time in the 2-worker image pool. The sanitize cache from #167355 runs after this encode and cannot skip it.

- `loadMediaFromRef` now loads through a new internal `loadReplayWebMedia`. It takes the same options and runs the same reads and checks as `loadWebMedia`, and it is not exported from the plugin SDK, so the public `loadWebMedia` signature is unchanged.
- The encode step moves out of `web-media.ts` into `web-media-image-encode.ts`, together with its verbose logging. For replay loads only, it remembers successful encodes in a process-level `LruCache` (the class #167355 uses). The key is the sha256 of the source bytes plus the byte cap, the compression grid sides and qualities, and the input pixel limit. Everything else that shapes the encode is constant, and the GIF, animated WebP, and preserve-original branches return before the lookup.
- Callers get copies and the cache keeps its own copy. Encode failures and results over the cap are never stored. The bound is 256 entries and 32 MiB of encoded bytes, about 60 downscaled 12 MP photos (one measured 530 KB).
- File reads, media-store resolution, `localRoots`, and the sandbox bridge still run on every call before the lookup. A hit requires already holding identical source bytes.
- Moving the encode step out keeps `web-media.ts` within its line-cap ratchet: the file shrinks (22 lines added, 37 removed).
- Accepted edge cases, also noted in the code: when the images replayed together outgrow 32 MiB, the cyclic scan misses every time and costs what `main` costs, plus a sha256 and a copy. If one search candidate fails transiently inside the encoder, the smaller candidate it settles on stays cached until eviction or restart, where `main` would retry on the next request.

`docs/reference/transcript-hygiene.md` describes the new cache next to the #167355 one.

## Evidence

Measured on `a44f088e7b9`. The branch is rebased onto `d220cac2b74`, which changes nothing in `src/media`, the replay runner files, or agent-core, and only rewords `docs/reference/transcript-hygiene.md` nearby.

### Running Gateway (QA Lab, isolated state)

`qa suite --provider-mode mock-openai --channel-driver qa-channel` starts a real Gateway child process on temporary state (its own home, state directory, and config), with qa-channel, the QA bus, and the mock OpenAI Responses provider. Two local files that are not part of this PR (below) drive it. A scenario attaches a 4032x3024 JPEG (3.97 MB) through the `agent` RPC, then sends a follow-up prompt. A mock-provider branch answers that prompt with six `read` tool calls and a final answer, so the follow-up turn makes 7 model requests. The scenario timestamps each model request from the mock request log and hashes the image bytes in every request body.

Same fixture and scenario on both trees, 2 alternating runs each, same Linux x64 machine, Node 26.11. `main` is `a44f088e7b9` and PR is this branch.

| Run | Photo turn (first encode) | Follow-up turn, 7 model requests | Gap between model requests |
| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |
| main 1 | 4.67 s | 7.06 s | 1,172 / 1,018 / 932 / 968 / 957 / 926 / 949 ms |
| main 2 | 4.65 s | 7.08 s | 1,185 / 998 / 957 / 957 / 968 / 953 / 951 ms |
| PR 1 | 4.74 s | 0.94 s | 313 / 109 / 66 / 88 / 65 / 86 / 86 ms |
| PR 2 | 4.67 s | 0.94 s | 315 / 110 / 69 / 88 / 64 / 92 / 87 ms |

Every model request in every run, on both trees, carried one image of 530,117 bytes with sha256 prefix `5693dc6d7309324c`, the same bytes the photo turn sent. The first gap includes run startup.

<details>
<summary>Local proof scenario (not part of this PR): <code>qa/scenarios/media/replay-photo-encode-proof.yaml</code></summary>

```yaml
title: Replayed photo encode proof

scenario:
  id: replay-photo-encode-proof
  surface: image-understanding
  coverage:
    primary: [media.image-summary-image-understanding]
  objective: Time a follow-up turn with several tool rounds after a 12 MP photo was attached, and record the image bytes every model request of that turn carried.
  successCriteria:
    - The follow-up turn finishes after its tool rounds.
    - Every model request of the follow-up turn carries the same image bytes.
  codeRefs:
    - src/media/web-media.ts
    - src/agents/embedded-agent-runner/run/images.ts
    - extensions/qa-lab/src/providers/mock-openai/server.ts
  execution:
    kind: flow
    suiteIsolation: isolated
    isolationReason: Times model request spacing on a dedicated Gateway.
    channel: qa-channel
    retryCount: 0
    summary: Attach a 12 MP photo, then run a follow-up turn with six tool rounds and record request timing and image digests.
    config:
      requiredProviderMode: mock-openai
      photoPath: .artifacts/replay-photo-proof/photo.jpg
      sessionKey: agent:qa:replay-photo-proof

flow:
  steps:
    - name: replays a stored photo across tool rounds
      actions:
        - assert:
            expr: "env.providerMode === config.requiredProviderMode"
            message: this proof requires mock-openai
        - call: waitForGatewayHealthy
          args:
            - ref: env
            - 60000
        - call: reset
        - set: photoBase64
          value:
            expr: "(await fs.readFile(config.photoPath)).toString('base64')"
        - set: describeRequests
          value:
            expr: |-
              async (afterCursor) => {
                const requests = await fetchJson(`${env.mock.baseUrl}/debug/requests?after=${afterCursor}`);
                const out = [];
                for (const request of requests) {
                  const raw = String(request.raw ?? '');
                  const images = [];
                  for (const match of raw.matchAll(/data:image\/[a-z]+;base64,([A-Za-z0-9+\/=]+)/g)) {
                    const bytes = Buffer.from(match[1], 'base64');
                    const digest = Buffer.from(await crypto.subtle.digest('SHA-256', bytes)).toString('hex');
                    images.push(`${bytes.length}:${digest.slice(0, 16)}`);
                  }
                  out.push({ cursor: request.cursor, requestKind: request.requestKind, imageInputCount: request.imageInputCount ?? 0, images });
                }
                return out;
              }
        - set: photoStartCursor
          value:
            expr: "(await fetchJson(`${env.mock.baseUrl}/debug/request-cursor`)).cursor"
        - set: photoTurnStarted
          value:
            expr: "Date.now()"
        - call: runAgentPrompt
          args:
            - ref: env
            - sessionKey:
                ref: config.sessionKey
              message: "REPLAY-PHOTO-PROOF-PHOTO: here is a photo from my phone."
              attachments:
                - mimeType: image/jpeg
                  fileName: photo.jpg
                  content:
                    ref: photoBase64
              timeoutMs: 180000
        - call: waitForCondition
          args:
            - lambda:
                expr: "state.getSnapshot().messages.find((message) => message.direction === 'outbound' && String(message.text ?? '').includes('REPLAY-PHOTO-PROOF-SEEN'))"
            - 180000
            - 100
        - set: photoTurn
          value:
            expr: "({ ms: Date.now() - photoTurnStarted, requests: await describeRequests(photoStartCursor) })"
        - set: followUpStartCursor
          value:
            expr: "(await fetchJson(`${env.mock.baseUrl}/debug/request-cursor`)).cursor"
        - set: followUp
          value:
            expr: |-
              await (async () => {
                const started = Date.now();
                const requestTimes = [];
                let seen = followUpStartCursor;
                let done = false;
                const watcher = (async () => {
                  while (!done) {
                    const { cursor } = await fetchJson(`${env.mock.baseUrl}/debug/request-cursor`);
                    if (cursor !== seen) {
                      requestTimes.push([Date.now() - started, cursor]);
                      seen = cursor;
                    }
                    await sleep(20);
