Closes #192086

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes Gateway shutdown waiting on OTLP metrics retries after the collector exits.

## Why This Change Was Made

The metric reader timeout does not cancel the OTLP exporter's retrying HTTP request. A shutdown-aware exporter applies a shorter request window only to new metric exports after shutdown starts.

## User Impact

Only new shutdown metric requests use a 1.5-second timeout by default; regular metric exports and logs/traces retain the standard 10-second request window. Signal-specific or shared `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_*_TIMEOUT` values take precedence. Without an exporter timeout override, an explicit shorter `OTEL_METRIC_EXPORT_TIMEOUT` also bounds shutdown metric requests. Requests already in flight keep their original timeout. The change is limited to metrics because #192086 reports a metrics-only deployment.

## Evidence

Before: on pinned base `e28ef12fb6a376672e9dbed98b46419012396bdd`, the same real-process proof took 11,553.99 ms in `service.stop()`. After: exact head `2e519e086eec1d69daaaac875cdabca68a297e42` took 1,615.36 ms. Both runs delivered one metric to a healthy local receiver, reported failure for the refused collector, and exited with code 0; startup and process-exit time were measured separately.

`pnpm test:extension diagnostics-otel extensions/diagnostics-otel/src/service.test.ts extensions/diagnostics-otel/src/service.otlp-export.test.ts` passed 2 files and 94 tests, including a healthy 1,650 ms in-flight metric request and shared/signal-specific timeout precedence. `pnpm check:test-lines-ratchet --base e28ef12fb6a376672e9dbed98b46419012396bdd` passed at 6,447,995 / 6,447,995 with a scoped 191-line allocation.

Latest-stable upgrade attempt against `openclaw@2026.10.10` did not reach upgrade assertions: baseline npm installation exceeded 1,200 seconds and left a missing package file. No upgrade result is claimed.

