## What Problem This Solves

CLI startup can add tools from the current workspace to child-process PATH when mise or XDG executable roots point into that workspace.

## User Impact

Workspace `.env` files cannot set `MISE_DATA_DIR` or `XDG_BIN_HOME`. CLI bootstrap rejects relative and workspace-contained package-manager candidates; trusted absolute tool directories outside the workspace remain supported. Operators using workspace-local roots must move those installations outside the workspace. Existing inherited `PATH` entries remain operator-controlled, including relative and workspace-local entries.

## Why This Change Was Made

Use the existing workspace dotenv filter and trusted package-manager-root validator. Validate the complete mise `shims` candidate so an absolute data directory ending in a significant space retains its exact name. This preserves the explicit-value contract introduced in https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/111258 and mise's existing precedence. No new configuration, state, or data migration is introduced.

The relative-root test now registers the candidate directories as existing, so it fails on the original vulnerable base. A new regression distinguishes an external `mise /shims` installation from its trimmed `mise/shims` neighbor. The documentation limits its protection claim to bootstrap-added candidates.

